hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well William James  essay the will to believe has to do with  a number of different kinds of belief  but in particular centers on religious  belief all of the argument and  exposition that he has been engaging in  throughout the essay as well as the many  examples that he has been providing are  culminating in his position in the end  that we we ought to be able and free to  decide for ourselves whether to believe  in what he calls the religious  hypothesis and there's there's certain  conditions involved with this he doesn't  want to embrace any sort of you know  what we might call rank superstition all  right he says that sad experience makes  me fear that some of you may still  shrink from radically saying with me in  abstract or that we have the right to  believe at our own risk any hypothesis  that is live enough to tempt our will  why because the freedom to believe what  we will can be applied to the case of  some patent superstition but James says  if we put that stuff aside when it comes  to religious questions  we do in fact have the right to decide  for ourselves and it's not irrational to  do so it's not any more irrational than  the blanket requirement that we subject  religious belief to something like  scientific verification or absolute  evidence provided through philosophical  argument James says that that would  itself be an irrational stance that many  people are imposing and he has as an  example of somebody who has this sort of  stance William Clifford who said that we  should never believe anything anywhere  on insufficient evidence that to do so  is wrong therefore believing in religion  is is wrong because there isn't any such  evidence so that's all sort of  background to where James is heading he  tells us something about the religious  belief or the religious hypothesis he  says that science says that things are  morality says some things are better  than other things  by contrast religion says essentially  two things what is it that religion  asserts here he's engaging in a bit of  abstraction about religions in general  says first she says that the best things  are the more eternal things the as he  calls them overlapping things the things  in the universe that throw the last  stone so to speak and say the final word  so the things that are really  determinative the things that are  foundational he says perfection is  eternal this phrase of Charles secretin  seems a good way of putting this first  affirmation of religion and notice what  he says an affirmation which obviously  cannot yet be verified scientifically at  all we can't use the means of science to  decide what in fact are the eternal  overlapping and determinative things it  doesn't mean that we can't have any  notion of these or we can't verify it in  other ways the second thing that he know  that he says the second proposition is  that we are better off even  now if we believe her first affirmation  to be true so we're better off if we  believe that statement to be true that  the best things are eternal overlapping  and determinative and so the one is a  claim about you know value order things  along those lines the second one is a  statement about what is good for us as  human beings now you may not recognize  most forms of religion in this rather  stripped-down way of expressing it but  this is what James thinks is really at  the essence of religion and it's good to  keep in mind that James himself had a  lot of experience with religion and  religious people and did in fact give  the lectures the varieties of religious  experience so he's not just doing  armchair pontificating he is he's saying  things that he thinks are really at the  core of genuine religious belief not  just mummery or you know religion and  social control or anything like that now  he goes on and he says let's consider  what the logical elements of this  situation are in case the religious  hypothesis in both its branches be  really true remember a option has to  have two hypotheses so if we want to say  that you know there's a religious  question right or that there's a  religious hypothesis that we have to  consider there have to be two sides to  the option what are the nature of these  so first he says that we must admit the  possibility at the outset if we're going  to discuss the question at all it must  involve a living option and here we  might think back to some of the  assertions that he made at the beginning  his crowd was a Protestant school where  he is giving this lecture and he says  that you know be a Christian or be an  agnostic these are live options for you  be a theosophist or be a Muslim not so  much now if you're in a place where  there's a lot of Muslim belief and  there's a you know mosque gia - right  down the road and you can study with  that you know  well that starts to become a live option  for you right or I don't know if you  hang out with the Theosophists wherever  they happen to be or whatever group  those are live options for you options  are live or dead relative to to us so  what might be a live option for me might  not actually be a live option for you  I'll mention just as an example of this  when I was in graduate school having  grown up in the Midwest where people you  know generally either were some you know  form of religion in the sense of  identifying with a religious  organization going to service is having  some beliefs or they you know had  rejected those beliefs or they were  agnostic or something like that it was a  genuine surprise to me to meet a fellow  graduate student who had grown up in  Southern California and had grown up in  such a secular environment that he  really was bemused about what religious  people thought they were up to I had  never really encountered what you might  call a cultural secularist like that  where it would just these weren't live  options for him at all you know for me I  could you know go back to the church or  reject the church or pick some other  denomination or you know embrace some  something else but for him none of those  things they actually made sense they  weren't live options so religion offers  us a possibility if it is indeed a live  option in the form that we're looking at  he also goes on and he says here we go  we see that religion offers itself as a  momentous option what is a momentous  option if we don't take the option we  miss out on something we can't go back  and point you to that point in time and  redo it or find similar options  occurring later on so he says we're  supposed to gain even now by our belief  and to lose by our non belief a certain  vital good that makes it a momentous  option but he says religion is also a  force  options so far as that good goes we  can't escape the issue by remaining  skeptical and waiting for more light  because although we do avoid error in  that way if religion be untrue we lose  the good if it be true just as certainly  as if we positively chose to disbelieve  it's as if a person should hesitate  indefinitely to ask a certain woman to  marry him because he was not perfectly  sure that she would prove an angel after  he brought her home very unrealistic  expectations of marriage right there  would he not cut himself off from that  particular angel possibility as  decisively as if he went and married  someone else so James goes on and he  says skepticism is not the avoidance of  option it's actually taking an option so  religion offers itself to at least some  of us under its its various guises or  modes as a living momentous and forced  option between hypotheses and it's good  to remember that James by this point has  established that when we have such a  genuine option such as that and it can't  be decided by intellectual means alone  he says that not only may we decided  based on our passional nature or a  volitional nature that is by willing by  deciding we must do so we must do so one  way or the other  and so James is making the argument that  to forbid that as many people who argue  against religion do or caution against  throwing yourself in or taking a leap of  faith are engaged in doing is actually a  illegitimate stance to take she says to  preach skepticism to us as duty until  sufficient evidence for reason for  religion be found is tantamount to  telling us when in presence of the  religious hypothesis that to yield to  our fear of it being err is wiser and  better than to yield to our hope that it  may be true and he says that what we  have an example of here is not the  intellect against passions but rather  the intellect being driven by one  passion against the other passion  the person who argues in favor of you  know don't make any commitments they're  driven by fear essentially fear of  falling into error or even of you  falling into error because it's not it's  not their belief that you're deciding  it's your belief what he says right say  says here we go if religion be true and  the evidence for it still be  insufficient I do not wish by putting  your extinguisher upon my nature which  feels to me as if I had after all some  business in this matter to forfeit my  sole chance in life of getting on the  winning side so that's a key aspect and  that takes us up to a certain point then  James introduces another wrinkle he  tells us that there's a further  dimension or claim of religion would  this indeed apply to all religions  perhaps not although people often want  to bring up you know examples of  Buddhism it's non-theistic well not  exactly  many forms of Buddhism are in fact  theistic and you know there is no self  true but there are persons if you're  praying to the Bodhisattva Guanyin or  trying to get yourself into the Pure  Land through faith and I meet a Buddha  that's that's a regular religious  hypotheses and that fits in with this  claim he says that one of the things  that religion tells us is that the more  perfect and more eternal aspect of the  universe is represented as having a  personal form a form we can interact  with the universe is no longer a mere it  to us but allow if we are religious and  and he says any relation that may be  possible from person to person might be  possible here notice he doesn't say is  possible might be possible so he says we  feel as if the appeal to religion to us  were made to our own active goodwill as  if evidence would be forever withheld  from us unless we met the hypothesis  way like we have to do with other  personal beings we can't just be in our  closed off bubble and demand that they  somehow permeate that and contact us we  have to go out to meet them as James  says earlier halfway when it comes to  matters of liking or loving or any other  personal relation to another being so  again he says that if the hypothesis  were true in all its parts including  this one then pure intellectualism with  its veto on our making willing advances  would be an absurdity  and some participation of our  sympathetic nature would be logically  required we would have to go out there  and extend ourselves and take a risk a  risk of falling into falsehood but the  risk that can also bring us into contact  with truth not merely possessing the  truth but being in relation to the truth  being in a personal relation with the  truth the last thing that he says is  that belief is measured by action so  because of that he says anyone who  forbids us to believe religion to be  true also forbids us to act as if we did  believe it to be true and then he goes  on and says the whole defense of  religious faith hinges upon action so  here's where James is giving us a sort  of pragmatic criterion for whether a  person really is embracing or acting  upon religious belief or faith does it  make any difference in their life in  ways that can be observed he says that  if the action required or inspired by  the religious hypothesis is in no way  different than that dictated by the  naturalistic hypothesis then religious  faith is a pure soup soup superfluity  better pruned away and controversy about  its legitimacy as a piece of idle  trifling unworthy of serious minds so if  the religious beliefs  doesn't actually make any difference in  a person's life in their behavior in  what they would do if they don't do  anything differently than they would if  they accepted the purely naturalistic  hypothesis that this is all we get and  there aren't any you know best things  that are eternal or any of that sort of  business let alone personal then it  doesn't really mean anything it doesn't  have any significance it's only when it  does lead to some modification of action  or a continuance of an action for  somebody who continues to live out a  religious faith  that it would have any significance so  James is providing us here with a  pragmatic argument for why it is that  not only should we be prepared to accept  religious belief on our own part or at  least to entertain it as a hypothesis  and then to try to see whether there's  anything there but also for the  legitimacy of other people's faith which  may have to do with very different live  options than those which appeal to us  you
 

